[Assistant]: Explanation: Let \(r\) be the inradius/apothem of the regular \(2n\)-gon. The alternate sides are tangent to this same incircle at \(n\) equally spaced points, so extending them forms a regular \(n\)-gon with the same inradius \(r\). A regular \(m\)-gon with inradius \(r\) has area \(A_m=m r^2\tan(\pi/m)\). Thus
\[
\frac{A_n}{A_{2n}}
=\frac{n r^2\tan(\pi/n)}{2n r^2\tan(\pi/(2n))}
=\frac{1}{1-\tan^2(\pi/(2n))}
=\frac{\cos^2(\pi/(2n))}{\cos(\pi/n)}
=\frac{1+\sec(\pi/n)}{2}.
\]
For \(n=3\), this gives \((1+\sec(\pi/3))/2=(1+2)/2=3/2\), as stated.
Answer: \(\displaystyle \frac{A_{\text{outer }n\text{-gon}}}{A_{\text{inner }2n\text{-gon}}}=\frac{1+\sec(\pi/n)}{2}\) equivalently \(\displaystyle \frac{1}{1-\tan^2(\pi/(2n))}\).
